"""Render the full-bank evaluation progress and, later, reviewed results."""
import json
from pathlib import Path
from html import escape as e
from site_navigation import project_tabs,experiment_subtabs
from build_answering_results import CSS,table,link,text
ROOT=Path(__file__).resolve().parents[1]
def writer_figure(cost):
 labels=[('summary','Summaries'),('legacy','Old structured notes'),('linked','Source notes')];bars=[]
 for i,(key,label) in enumerate(labels):
  value=cost[key]['generated_tokens'];y=44+i*55;width=430*value/1000000
  bars.append(f'<text x="8" y="{y+17}" font-size="15" fill="currentColor">{e(label)}</text><rect x="205" y="{y}" width="{width:.1f}" height="25" rx="3" fill="'+['#7795b8','#b67a57','#378a78'][i]+f'"/><text x="{215+width:.1f}" y="{y+17}" font-size="15" fill="currentColor">{value:,}</text>')
 return '<figure id="fig-earlier-tokens"><svg viewBox="0 0 770 235" role="img" aria-label="Earlier generated tokens: summaries477979; old structured notes872613; source notes346754" style="width:100%;height:auto"><text x="205" y="24" font-size="15" fill="currentColor">Earlier generated tokens over 26 meetings</text>'+''.join(bars)+'<text x="205" y="223" font-size="13" fill="currentColor">All writer and revision-check outputs counted once per method.</text></svg><figcaption><strong>Figure 1.</strong> Earlier generation cost for the three note methods; exact costs appear in Table 4.</figcaption></figure>'
def main():
 d=json.loads((ROOT/'research/full-answering/progress.json').read_text());body='<header class="hero"><p class="small">Full-bank evaluation · '+e(d['updated'])+'</p><h1>S1 and S2 on all 105 questions</h1></header><section id="findings"><h2>Current findings and progress</h2><ul>'+''.join('<li>'+e(x['text'])+' '+link(x['url'],'Details')+'</li>' for x in d['brief'])+'</ul><p>'+link('../todo.html','Project checklist')+' · '+link('../daily-log.html','Daily log')+' · '+link('structured-notes.html','Completed note-optimization pilot')+'</p></section>'
 body+='<section id="coverage"><h2>1. Evaluation set and coverage</h2><p>All 105 existing questions are retained across 26 meetings and earnings calls. The bank includes the 18 questions used to refine source notes; 44 questions come from 15 other meetings and will also be analyzed separately. Each S1 model receives the same source recording and unchanged, voice-matched synthesized question, starting with “Assistant,”. The source prefixes total 30.71 hours per model in the full-input condition.</p>'+table(1,'Execution and validation are tracked separately.',['Work','Current status','Accuracy status'],[[e(x['work']),e(x['status']),e(x['validation'])] for x in d['coverage']])+'</section>'
 body+='<section id="s1"><h2>2. Duplex-only models</h2><p>DuplexOmni, Moshi and MiniCPM-o 4.5 process their native audio packets in order. Model outputs enter the native conversation state before the next packet. The three conditions supply only the question, the preceding four minutes plus the question, or all preceding audio plus the question. Question timing within the native packets is held fixed. The main output is the model’s native text during the 19.2 seconds following the supplied input.</p><p>All input audio is processed in the full-input condition. Retained history follows each model’s capacity: Moshi uses its native 3,000-step attention window (about 240 seconds); MiniCPM-o 4.5 uses its native decoder with sliding-window removal off; DuplexOmni retains all turns until its context or audio-count limit and then removes the oldest complete turns to half capacity. Every removal is logged. Malformed native packet replies are retained verbatim in conversation history, with incomplete fields recorded separately, so one unfinished packet does not end the meeting. Empty answers, incomplete inputs, context-limit events and software failures receive separate counts.</p>'+table(2,'S1 conditions; each row is run for all three models.',['Condition','Audio input','Questions per model'],[['Request only','Inserted spoken question, with native packet alignment.','105'],['Recent history','Latest 240 seconds of original discussion plus question.','105'],['Full input','All original audio through the insertion time plus question.','105']])+'<p>DuplexOmni uses constrained listening during the original discussion and inserted question: the ASR and other native fields are decoded for every packet, outward speech is suppressed in the saved listening state, and answering is enabled at the known end of the supplied question audio. This gives DuplexOmni the question-end time; it does not test autonomous response timing. Moshi and MiniCPM retain their native streaming output and cache behavior.</p><p>Before scaling DuplexOmni, the JSON and binary endpoints produced identical native fields and saved history for all 104 packet records in two short controls. Waveform synthesis can therefore be omitted for this text evaluation. Stopping decoding immediately after ASR changed later responses and was excluded. The selected waveform-decoding and resampling cache also preserved all 104 native field records, with fresh model decoding on every packet.</p></section>'
 body+='<section id="s2"><h2>3. Text S2 conditions</h2><p>The readers are Qwen3-30B-A3B-Instruct-2507 and Qwen3-30B-A3B-Thinking-2507. A common Instruct writer generates the saved summaries and notes for both readers before seeing any future question. The reader receives the complete available transcript, the applicable saved notes and the question. Thinking uses seeds 23 and 47; Instruct uses greedy decoding.</p>'+table(3,'Nine ground-truth-transcript conditions; each uses all 105 questions.',['Condition','Saved material at question time','Purpose'],[['Reactive','None.','Baseline without earlier reasoning.'],['Reactive + focused prompt','None.','Measure the effect of the shorter, evidence-focused reader instruction.'],['Summaries · all snapshots','Every valid earlier summary that fits.','Reproduce accumulated-summary prompting.'],['Summary · latest snapshot','Only the latest valid summary.','Remove duplicated older summaries without changing writer work.'],['Structured notes · all snapshots','Every valid earlier structured snapshot that fits.','Reproduce the original structured-note method.'],['Structured notes · latest snapshot','Only the latest valid structured snapshot.','Test whether older snapshots help.'],['Source notes','One bounded state of selected original speech and relationships.','Test incremental, deduplicated source selection.'],['Source notes + focused prompt','The same source state.','Test focused reading with saved evidence.'],['Source notes without links + focused prompt','Identical selected source speech; relationship links removed.','Isolate the contribution of links between statements.']])+'<p>Source-note updates use new transcript segments, ten seconds of earlier reference speech and one current state, with at most 64 selected segments, 20 visible links and 4,096 compact-JSON tokens. The existing 25–40 second update schedule is within the requested 10–40 second range. Summaries and old structured notes use the original full-prefix and earlier-snapshot prompts. Earlier generation costs are shared when conditions reuse the same notes.</p><p>Both readers have a common 49,152-token input limit within a 65,536-token context. If accumulated snapshots exceed that input limit, the oldest snapshots are omitted and their IDs are recorded; the transcript is retained. No reference answer enters the writer or reader input.</p></section>'
 if d.get('writer_cost'):
  c=d['writer_cost'];rows=[[label,f"{c[key]['calls']:,}",f"{c[key]['input_tokens']:,}",f"{c[key]['generated_tokens']:,}"] for key,label in [('summary','Summaries'),('legacy','Original structured notes'),('linked','Source notes')]]
  body+='<section id="writer-cost"><h3>Completed earlier note generation</h3><p>All 26 meetings have completed their question-blind note runs. Source notes use 346,754 earlier output tokens, compared with 872,613 for the original structured notes, a 60.3% reduction (Figure 1). The source total includes 239 revision-check calls. These are earlier note-generation costs; full answer accuracy and post-request latency remain pending.</p>'+writer_figure(c)+table(4,'Earlier writer work over 26 meetings, counted once per policy before reuse by readers and seeds.',['Method','Calls','Logical input tokens','Earlier output tokens'],rows)+'<p>All 1,277 source-note states pass checks for exact copied speech, speaker labels, times and the declared memory budget. Six summary snapshots and 18 original structured-note snapshots cite transcript IDs that were unavailable at their update time; those snapshots are excluded from reader inputs and their generation costs remain counted. These checks cover source copying and availability; relationship meanings are evaluated separately.</p><details><summary>Duplication check over all 105 question inputs</summary><p>Before applying the reader input limit, accumulated old snapshots contain a mean 362.1 note entries per question, of which 348.6 repeat an earlier normalized fact text. The current source state contains a mean 48.3 selected speech segments and no exact repeated text. This counts exact repetitions after case and punctuation normalization; accuracy and preservation of important revisions require the reader evaluation. '+link('../research/full-answering/notes-audit.json','All 105 duplication checks and state-audit counts')+'</p></details></section>'
 body+='<section id="latency"><h2>4. Post-request latency and token cost</h2><p>The primary timing comparison uses isolated, serial Thinking requests for all 105 ground-truth-transcript questions, with seed 23. It measures time to the first answer text and time to the completed output. The prefix cache is cleared before each request and condition order is shuffled. Batched accuracy-run times are retained as throughput measurements.</p><p>These timings cover the reader with notes already available; they do not include ASR or establish that background work finishes before a live request. Native thinking tokens count reasoning generated after the request; amortized-thinking tokens count earlier note generation and revision checks. Answer output and all earlier input/output costs are reported separately. Instruct does not expose a separate thinking channel. A reduction in token count will be called a latency improvement only when the measured time also falls and answer quality is retained.</p><p>The planned figures show paired per-question changes, accuracy against post-request thinking and wall time, and total earlier versus request-time generated tokens. Meeting-level confidence intervals will keep repeated questions and seeds from being treated as independent meetings.</p></section>'
 qd=json.loads((ROOT/'research/full-answering/timing-qualification.json').read_text());qids=list(dict.fromkeys(x['question_id'] for x in qd['cases']));qr=[]
 for qid in qids:
  pair={x['condition']:x for x in qd['cases'] if x['question_id']==qid};a=pair['reactive_guided'];b=pair['linked_guided'];qr.append([e(a['question']),str(a['native_thinking_tokens']),f"{a['latency_seconds']['first_answer_text']:.2f}",str(b['native_thinking_tokens']),f"{b['latency_seconds']['first_answer_text']:.2f}"])
 body+='<section id="timing-check"><h3>Timing qualification: two evaluation questions</h3><p>Four actual answers qualify the streaming measurements. Source checking confirms the necessary facts in these answers. Source notes reduce response time for one question and increase it for the other; the full 105-question comparison is pending.</p>'+table(5 if d.get('writer_cost') else 4,'Qualification examples; latency is seconds from request submission to first answer text.',['Question','Reactive thinking tokens','Reactive latency (s)','Source-note thinking tokens','Source-note latency (s)'],qr)
 for x in qd['cases']:
  body+='<details><summary>'+e(x['question'])+' · '+('Source notes' if x['condition']=='linked_guided' else 'Reactive')+'</summary>'+text(x['answer'])+'<p><strong>Original speech:</strong></p><ul>'+''.join('<li>'+e(s['speaker']+': '+s['text'])+'</li>' for s in x['evidence'])+'</ul></details>'
 body+='<p>'+link('../research/full-answering/timing-qualification.json','Exact final answers and recorded timestamps')+'</p></section>'
 body+='<section id="asr"><h2>5. Generated transcripts and speaker identities</h2><p>The full extension includes Parakeet and Whisper, causal audio prefixes and stable speaker labels. Automatic requester matching is compared with a separately labeled diagnostic that uses past annotations to map speaker identities. Notes retain stable anonymous labels; the diagnostic supplies a consistent identity legend at question time.</p><p>Question text is held fixed across S2 conditions; ASR replaces the prior meeting speech. The four primary ASR comparisons use the same original reader prompt: reactive, all summaries, all structured-note snapshots and source notes. Word times assign recognized words to speakers and prevent future speech from entering earlier updates.</p><p>The variable-speaker pipeline completed six earnings-call prefix tests without receiving the reference speaker count. It estimated six speakers in the first call and five in the second; the reference annotations contain six and nine labels respectively. Full-bank ASR, WER and speaker-identity checks remain pending.</p></section>'
 body+='<section id="evaluation"><h2>6. Reference and scoring checks</h2><p>A new Thinking-model audit read the complete available transcript for all 105 questions. Codex checked the nine flagged chronology, identity and scope claims against the original speech and froze the references for scorer qualification. <a href="../research/full-answering/reference-flag-review.json">Source checks for all nine flags</a>.</p><p>The main score checks the minimum facts needed to answer the question and reports material unsupported claims separately. A supported-correct answer supplies the necessary facts and has no material unsupported claim; optional qualifications are recorded separately. All history conditions use the same full-prefix reference, so request-only accuracy measures how often a model gives the target answer without the earlier discussion. Response-type counts separately track refusals, acknowledgements and empty replies.</p><p>The current evaluator agrees with all 50 reviewed development controls on required facts and unsupported claims, including the individual ownership checks. Codex reviewed the final control outputs, and the full S2 reader is released. Full-bank accuracy still requires completed outputs and source inspection of real answers.</p><details><summary>Evaluation prompts, code rules and actual checks</summary><p>Qwen3-30B-A3B-Instruct first compares the answer with the verified minimum facts. It consults the full causal transcript for additional claims or alternative explanations that the short reference cannot resolve. Code handles empty replies, replies containing only a refusal or promise to check, evaluation-only speaker-label mapping and claims of completed external lookups. A targeted check distinguishes such lookups from a supported meeting report. Reading supplied notes is allowed.</p><p>For example, “D.” answers who volunteered to take notes; “B.” names the wrong person; “Let me check the folder status for you” leaves the question unanswered. Seven minutes is sufficient for the presentation-time question, while seven minutes plus a claimed live-calendar lookup includes an unsupported action. The 50 controls were used during scorer development; they measure agreement with Codex source reviews. Real answer gains, losses and a reproducible sample from other questions will receive further source review. '+link('../research/full-answering/scorer-protocol.json','Exact evaluator prompts, code-rule descriptions and all 50 expected and predicted labels')+'</p></details></section>'
 body+='<section id="resources"><h2>7. Compute and next steps</h2><p>Each GPU job uses one 80 GB GPU, with all of its model components on GPU 0. MiniCPM, the original Moshi run and the note writer use H100 SXM GPUs; the Moshi continuation, scorer calibration and accuracy-only readers can use A100 SXM4 GPUs. The isolated latency study uses one H100 SXM throughout. DuplexOmni qualification used one A100 with the complete S1 stack; full native-text workers use separate A100 or H100 GPUs for the Thinker, with each evaluation case assigned to one worker. Dependent jobs reuse a lane, with at most four GPUs active. ASR runs after native inference frees its lane, and response-audio synthesis follows the completed native-text workers. vLLM supplies BF16 inference, CUDA graphs and prefix caching for text models.</p><ul>'+''.join('<li>'+e(x)+'</li>' for x in d['next_steps'])+'</ul></section>'
 page='<!doctype html><html lang="en"><head><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1"><meta name="color-scheme" content="light dark"><meta name="description" content="Full-bank S1 and S2 comparison: answer accuracy, post-request latency and thinking-token cost."><title>Full-bank S1 and S2 evaluation · AI2AI Duplex</title><link rel="stylesheet" href="../assets/site.css"><style>'+CSS+'</style></head><body><a class="skip-link" href="#main">Skip to content</a><header class="site-nav"><div class="nav-inner"><a class="brand" href="../index.html">AI2AI Duplex</a>'+project_tabs('experiments','../')+'</div></header><div class="container collection-nav">'+experiment_subtabs('full-answering')+'</div><main id="main" class="container results">'+body+'</main></body></html>\n';(ROOT/'experiments/full-answering-results.html').write_text(page)
if __name__=='__main__':main()
